---
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import CommandPager from '../../components/CommandPager.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';

const title = 'scope';
const description =
  'Scope rules to file patterns so the right context loads in the right files. Nothing global unless it really is global.';
---

<Doc
  title={title}
  description={description}
  section="commands"
  sourceFile="skills/groundwork/commands/scope.md"
  templateFiles={['skills/groundwork/templates/cursor-rule.mdc.template']}
>
  <p class="doc-lede">
    Move rules to where they belong. Frontend conventions apply to frontend
    files, SQL conventions apply to migrations, infra conventions apply to
    infra. The agent's attention budget is finite; spend it on what matters.
  </p>

  <h2>When to use it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Multi-stack repo: frontend + backend, app + infra, multiple languages.</li>
    <li>Monorepo with packages of distinct character.</li>
    <li>Rules keep firing where they should not (frontend conventions on a Python script).</li>
    <li><code>CLAUDE.md</code> is past 200 lines from trying to cover every area.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What it produces</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cursor</strong>: <code>.cursor/rules/&lt;area&gt;.mdc</code> with a <code>globs</code> list.</li>
    <li><strong>Copilot</strong>: <code>.github/instructions/&lt;area&gt;.instructions.md</code> with <code>applyTo</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Claude Code / Codex</strong>: nested <code>CLAUDE.md</code> / <code>AGENTS.md</code> at directory boundaries.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The top-level file shrinks in the same pass. Rules duplicated into scoped files are removed.</p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Map the repo.</strong> Globs top-level directories. Notes primary languages and conventions that look different across them.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask which areas need rules.</strong> Multi-select with detected candidates: <code>src/components/</code>, <code>src/api/</code>, <code>db/migrations/</code>, <code>infra/</code>, etc. Plus harnesses.</li>
    <li><strong>For each area, gather rules.</strong> Sample files, propose 3 to 5 conventions with Preferred / Avoid pairs, confirm.</li>
    <li><strong>Write the scoped files.</strong> Same body, different wrappers per harness.</li>
    <li><strong>Reconcile.</strong> Top-level file gets leaner. Rules now in scoped files are removed or pointered.</li>
    <li><strong>Verify.</strong> Pick one file per scoped area and confirm the right rules apply.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Cursor rule types</h2>
  <p>Use the narrowest one that works: a rule costs tokens on every request it matches. Names are Cursor's own, from its rule-type dropdown.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Frontmatter</th><th>Fires when</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Always Apply</td><td><code>alwaysApply: true</code></td><td>Every request</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Apply to Specific Files</td><td><code>alwaysApply: false</code> + <code>globs</code></td><td>A matching file is in play</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Apply Intelligently</td><td><code>alwaysApply: false</code> + <code>description</code>, no <code>globs</code></td><td>The agent judges it relevant</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Apply Manually</td><td>neither <code>globs</code> nor <code>description</code></td><td>Only when <code>@</code>-mentioned</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    A plain <code>.md</code> file in <code>.cursor/rules/</code> is ignored; the
    <code>.mdc</code> extension is required.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>On body length.</strong> Cursor documents one limit: keep a rule
    under 500 lines, and split larger ones into composable rules. It publishes
    <strong>no</strong> per-type word or token budget, so Groundwork does not
    quote one as if it were a product constraint. The useful discipline is
    narrower globs, not shorter prose — a 300-line rule scoped to
    <code>db/migrations/**</code> costs nothing when editing React. If a rule is
    long <em>and</em> broad, split it by path, or move long examples into
    <code>docs/agents/&lt;area&gt;.md</code>.
  </p>

  <CommandPager slug="scope" />
</Doc>
